{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789833922,"nonce":"70poCkIaAQs6ibDa5T5VBlo7WNo=","title":"Start-up compiled and optimised every reachable function before the first instruction ran","message":"The ladder says a body compiles when its bead crosses the baseline\nthreshold or a loop asks, but `compile_module` ran the whole HIR\npipeline over the program (36 to 98 ms on the speed-center kernels,\nNUC) and Cranelift-compiled every function not in the cold-only or\nlibrary-callable set (35 to 67 ms), then handed the interpreter code it\nwould run for 20 calls before using. Every lazy function also got a\nstub at load (313 stubs, 11 ms), and the per-run drop-insert facts were\nrebuilt for a pipeline with nothing left to optimise.\n\nPer process the fixed cost was 130 to 340 ms against PyPy's 50 to 90:\non richards (80 ms of work) three quarters of the wall.\n\nNow:\n- every reachable body waits for its call; the interpreter runs the\n  first ones and the warm-up thread compiles ahead, the program's own\n  functions first (entry points, then breadth first over direct calls),\n  then the library's;\n- a program function is optimised alone at its first compile, in one\n  scratch copy of the module; the module-wide facts the passes need\n  (drop-insert ownership facts, the inliner's call cycles) are built\n  once (`OptCache`), since optimising a body changes none of them; the\n  scratch is dropped once the warm-up thread has compiled everything;\n- a lazy function's stub is emitted when compiled code first reads its\n  cell, and on demand for a host or the interpreter asking for its\n  address (`entry_or_stub`);\n- the optimised body is kept only for a ladder with an LLVM tier;\n- `run_interp_safe_opts` returns at once when no function needs it;\n- tick callbacks no longer copy every function body at load;\n- `ZYNTAX_DISABLE_LAZY=1` compiles everything up front, as before.\n\nparse+compile on the NUC (min of 5): fannkuch 94 -\u003e 16 ms compile,\nnbody 172 -\u003e 28, richards 140 -\u003e 28, meteor 207 -\u003e 29, nqueens 237 -\u003e 34.\nThe first iteration of a kernel is slower (it runs interpreted until\nits loops transfer), the 50-iteration means are unchanged.\n\nKnown limits: the warm-up thread compiles every lazy function\neventually, called or not; the interpreter still blocks on a first-call\ncompile at the 20th call rather than continuing meanwhile.","files":null}]}